.TH KILL 1 "2026-10-15" "SlateOS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kill \- stop a process, or send it a signal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPID\fR...
.br
.B kill
\fB\-l\fR [\fISIGNAL\fR]
.br
.B kill
\fB\-L\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
procps-ng's \fBkill\fR, with SlateOS's three ways to stop a program.
.PP
With no signal named, each process is asked to \fIterminate\fR: to stop
now and cleanly, asking nothing.  A SlateOS program that answers such
requests is asked by message; any other process \(em every program ported
from Linux \(em is sent SIGTERM, as on Linux.
.PP
A signal named with \fB\-\fR\fISIGNAL\fR, \fB\-s\fR or \fB\-q\fR is sent
exactly as named, the Linux way, whoever the process is.
.PP
A negative \fIPID\fR is a process group, which is only ever signalled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\fR\fISIGNAL\fR, \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-signal\fR \fISIGNAL\fR
Send \fISIGNAL\fR, by name (\fBHUP\fR, \fBSIGKILL\fR, \fBRTMIN+3\fR) or
number.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-queue\fR \fIVALUE\fR
Send the signal with \fBsigqueue\fR(3), carrying \fIVALUE\fR.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR[=\fISIGNAL\fR]
List the signal names, or translate one between name and number.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-table\fR
List the signal names and numbers in a table.
.TP
\fB\-\-close\fR
Ask each process to close, as its own close command does: it may ask
about unsaved work, and may decline.  A process that does not answer
requests is not touched, and \fBkill\fR says so.
.TP
\fB\-\-terminate\fR
Ask each process to stop now, asking nothing (the default).
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
End each process at once (SIGKILL).
.TP
\fB\-\-via=message\fR, \fB\-\-via=signal\fR
Send the request only by message (a process that does not answer is
refused) or only by signal.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR \fIREQUEST\fR
If a process is still there \fIMS\fR milliseconds later, send it
\fIREQUEST\fR: \fBclose\fR, \fBterminate\fR or \fBforce\fR.  May be
given more than once.
.PP
The request options are recognised only when spelled in full.
\fBpkill\fR(1) and \fBkillall\fR(1) take the same ones.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every process was reached, 1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
kill 1234                       Ask 1234 to terminate (SIGTERM if it
                                does not answer requests)
kill --close 1234               Ask 1234 to close, as its window would
kill --close --timeout 10000 terminate --timeout 5000 force 1234
                                Close; terminate after 10 s; force after 5 s more
kill -9 1234                    Send SIGKILL
kill -s HUP 5678                Send SIGHUP
kill -l                         List the signal names
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkill (1),
.BR killall (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR signal (7)
